 "director": {
  "mode": "hand",
  "notes": "43 carries Al-Muyassar's «من ترك الأولى والأكمل» (the reproach is about the better course, not a sin). 46:7-11 keeps the creed point of both tafsirs: held back by decree «وإن كان أمرهم به شرعا». Two paths for the truthful and the liars, coins for «بأموالهم», wind for «يترددون», a house for «اقعدوا». No motif on the Prophet ﷺ. STAGING v4: c1 (43) stays ambient: Allah addresses the Prophet about his own permission to them (nothing stands in for him); the mood moves from geometric to night_sky (Allah's direct address, no central light source) and the background is quiet over the whole verse; the two-paths plate stays on 43:10 (the truthful and the liars). c2 (44-45): a town by day. 44 is about the believers (both tafsirs: they do not ask leave from striving): three believers stand on the left and do nothing (no march, no camel, no weapon: the striving itself is never drawn); coins plate on 44:10 (wealth), geometric plate on the Name 44:12 (His knowledge of the God-fearing). 45: three men (the hypocrites) walk in from the right on 45:1; they never speak (their asking leave is addressed to the Prophet, so nobody speaks toward an empty side); they turn their backs on 45:9 (doubt), face again on 45:11 and turn away again on 45:14 (hesitating back and forth: both tafsirs, in doubt and confusion). c3 (46): the same three men in the town. 46:1-6 is a counterfactual (if they had wanted to go out they would have prepared): nothing is prepared and no mount is drawn. A geometric plate on 46:8 for His dislike of their going (His decree; the Name follows); they sit down on 46:11 (held back: both tafsirs, by decree) and a house plate on 46:12-13 (stay behind). The sick, the weak, the women and the children of the gloss are not drawn. The cover now shows the men sitting in the town (46:13), matching the title about those who stayed behind."
 },
